is localized and labs don't confirm acute toxicity"], "alarm_features": ["Hypoglycemia history with prior hospitalizations"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Autoimmune Myopathy (Dermatomyositis/Polymyositis)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25, "posterior": 0.3048776769784427, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Facial/upper-body rash is pathognomonic for dermatomyositis but not polymyositis. This weakly supports B1 while being neutral/weakly against others due to limited specificity in the current scenario."], "evidence_against": ["Normal CK levels reduce (but don't exclude) autoimmune myopathy likelihood. Both B1 and B4 are weakly disfavored, while B2 and B3 remain neutral.", "Normal AST/ALT reduces (but does not exclude) chronic inflammatory myopathies. Both B1 and B4 are moderately disfavored while B2/3 remain neutral."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there evidence of proximal muscle weakness?", "Are there characteristic cutaneous signs of dermatomyositis on the face/arms?", "Is there a symmetric pattern of muscle involvement?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)/free T4", "Muscle enzyme panel (CPK, aldolase)", "Corticosteroid-responsive symptoms/physical findings"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Electrolyte Disturbance with Myopathy (Chronic Hypokalemia)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2, "posterior": 0.2439021415827542, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Chronic hypokalaemia can cause muscular weakness but typically without associated rash. This moderately supports B2 while being weakly against B1 and B3 due to lack of cutaneous/endocrine correlation in labs."], "evidence_against": ["Cutaneous findings and body hair changes are inconsistent with purely electrolyte-based myopathy. B2 is weakly disfavored while B3 (thyroid dysfunction) gains weak support.", "Isolated hypokalemia is more consistent with dietary/acute causes, while thyroid dysfunction often involves other metabolic abnormalities. Mildly supports B3 over B2."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Any history of diuretic use or excessive fluid loss?", "Are electrolyte abnormalities persistent or episodic?", "Do symptoms improve with potassium supplementation?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["24-hour urinary potassium", "Serum magnesium", "Repeat electrolyte testing post-hypoglycemic episodes"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.15, "interrupt_relevance": 0.45, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Endocrine/Diabetic Complications with Myopathy", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15, "posterior": 0.32926789113671817, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Cutaneous findings and body hair changes are inconsistent with purely electrolyte-based myopathy. B2 is weakly disfavored while B3 (thyroid dysfunction) gains weak support.", "Isolated hypokalemia is more consistent with dietary/acute causes, while thyroid dysfunction often involves other metabolic abnormalities. Mildly supports B3 over B2."], "evidence_against": ["Chronic hypokalaemia can cause muscular weakness but typically without associated rash. This moderately supports B2 while being weakly against B1 and B3 due to lack of cutaneous/endocrine correlation in labs."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Are th