{"state": {"case_id": "MB_20", "timestep": 1, "case_summary": "A 57-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with weakness for the past 6 months with symptoms that have worsened recently. She feels fatigued, depressed, and has gained 10 pounds which she attributes to feeling too weak to go to the gym. She was recently treated for an ear infection with an antibiotic which she completed 3 weeks ago, and experienced a rash which she believes was an allergic reaction to her treatment. The patient has a medical history of diabetes mellitus that is well-controlled with insulin and metformin. She has been admitted multiple times for hypoglycemia secondary to missing meals. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 127/68 mmHg, pulse is 80/min, respirations are 17/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for 2/5 strength in the patient's upper and lower extremities, sparse fine hair on her body, and a rash on her face and arms. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L K+: 3.3 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L HCO3-: 25 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 70 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.2 mg/dL AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L A muscle biopsy is obtained as seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this patient's presentation?\n\nOptions:\nA. Dermatomyositis\nB. Hypokalemia\nC. Hypothyroidism\nD. Inclusion body myositis\nE. Polymyositis\n", "root": {"label": "Chronic Progressive Generalized Muscular Weakness with Hypokalemia and Possible Myopathy (Muscle Biopsy)", "time_course": "chronic", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["Weakness for 6 months with recent worsening", "2/5 strength in upper and lower extremities", "Hypokalemia (K+ 3.3 mEq/L)", "Muscle biopsy obtained but not described"], "excluded_candidates": ["Autoimmune myopathy: lacks hallmark antibodies or inflammatory markers in labs", "Antibiotic-induced myopathy: possible but lower priority as rash is localized and labs don't confirm acute toxicity"], "alarm_features": ["Hypoglycemia history with prior hospitalizations"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Autoimmune Myopathy (Dermatomyositis/Polymyositis)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25, "posterior": 0.3048776769784427, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Facial/upper-body rash is pathognomonic for dermatomyositis but not polymyositis. This weakly supports B1 while being neutral/weakly against others due to limited specificity in the current scenario."], "evidence_against": ["Normal CK levels reduce (but don't exclude) autoimmune myopathy likelihood. Both B1 and B4 are weakly disfavored, while B2 and B3 remain neutral.", "Normal AST/ALT reduces (but does not exclude) chronic inflammatory myopathies. Both B1 and B4 are moderately disfavored while B2/3 remain neutral."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there evidence of proximal muscle weakness?", "Are there characteristic cutaneous signs of dermatomyositis on the face/arms?", "Is there a symmetric pattern of muscle involvement?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)/free T4", "Muscle enzyme panel (CPK, aldolase)", "Corticosteroid-responsive symptoms/physical findings"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Electrolyte Disturbance with Myopathy (Chronic Hypokalemia)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2, "posterior": 0.2439021415827542, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Chronic hypokalaemia can cause muscular weakness but typically without associated rash. This moderately supports B2 while being